  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14173511/assets/4b89ec998b55eacb8e5de3bf0c22aa94d351232f38459c17640956f96e939bac.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $KN=LN$ and $KP=LP.$",
  "worked_solution": "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 8x-6 & =7x+1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
